{"name":"Gleam","hex":"#b9cba3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gleam","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An appearance of light, especially one which is indistinct or small, or short-lived.","An indistinct sign of something; a glimpse or hint."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Chiefly in conjunction with an adverb: to cause (light) to shine.","To shine, especially in an indistinct or intermittent manner; to glisten, to glitter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gleam"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gleam (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Gleam  is a live album recorded in 1975 by jazz trumpeter Freddie Hubbard. It was released as a double LP on CBS/Sony and features a live performance recorded in Tokyo by Hubbard, Carl Randall, George Cables, Henry Franklin, Carl Burnett and Buck Clark."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/2f/Gleam_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gleam_(album)","wikidata":"Q5567377"},{"title":"The Gleam","blocks":[{"text":"The Gleam is an album by saxophonist Steve Lacy's Sextet, recorded in 1986 and released on the Swedish Silkheart label."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/40/The_Gleam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gleam","wikidata":"Q7736737"},{"title":"Gleam (programming language)","blocks":[{"text":"Gleam is a general-purpose, concurrent, functional, high-level programming language that compiles to Erlang or JavaScript source code."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Gleam_Lucy.svg/330px-Gleam_Lucy.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gleam_(programming_language)","wikidata":"Q66370905"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"gleam":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https: